### Sub-Threat Scenarios by Domain

#### 1. Misuse Domain

| Sub-Threat          | Scenario Description                                                          |
| ------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Prompt Injection    | Hidden instructions bypass safeguards to execute unintended, malicious actions|
| LLM Jailbreaking    | User manipulates prompts to elicit prohibited content or reveal system rules. |
| Deepfake Generation | System is used to create convincing, fabricated audio or visual content.      |
| Disinformation Ops  | Model is leveraged to generate false narratives or propaganda at scale.       |
| Bot Abuse           | Automated agents exploit the AI to perform spamming, scraping, or fraud.      |
| Shadow AI Usage     | Employees use unvetted public AI tools, exposing corporate data.              |

#### 2. Poisoning Domain

| Sub-Threat                 | Scenario Description                                                             |
| -------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Targeted Data Poisoning    | Malicious examples are inserted to create a specific, targeted model failure.    |
| Model Backdooring          | A hidden trigger in model input causes a specific, malicious output.             |
| Tainted Open-Source Models | A pre-trained model from a public hub contains a pre-embedded vulnerability.      |
| Logic Corruption           | Training data is manipulated to teach the model an incorrect, harmful rule.      |
| Poisoned ML Libraries      | A compromised dependency in the MLOps toolchain injects malicious code.          |

#### 3. Privacy Domain

| Sub-Threat              | Scenario Description                                                                    |
| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Model Inversion         | Reconstructing sensitive training data samples (e.g., faces) from model outputs.        |
| Membership Inference    | Determining if a specific individual's data was used in the model's training set.       |
| PII Leakage from LLMs   | Model inadvertently reveals sensitive PII it memorized during its training.             |
| Sensitive Data Leakage  | User queries inadvertently cause the model to leak confidential business information.   |
| Inference Eavesdropping | An attacker observes model inputs/outputs to infer sensitive user information.          |

#### 4. Adversarial Domain

| Sub-Threat                      | Scenario Description                                                          |
| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Evasion Attacks                 | A malicious input is slightly altered to be misclassified by the model.       |
| Adversarial Patch/Image         | A visible pattern is applied to an object to fool a computer vision system.   |
| Model Denial of Service (DoS)   | Crafting inputs that cause extreme computational load, freezing the model.     |
| Adversarial Reprogramming       | Crafting inputs that repurpose the model to perform an unintended task.       |
| Oracle and Extraction Attacks   | Querying a model repeatedly to steal its parameters or recreate its logic.    |

#### 5. Biases Domain

| Sub-Threat             | Scenario Description                                                        |
| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Representational Harm  | Model reinforces negative stereotypes against a protected demographic group.  |
| Allocational Harm      | Model unfairly denies opportunities or resources (e.g., loans, jobs).       |
| Data Imbalance Bias    | Skewed training data causes poor performance for underrepresented groups.   |
| Proxy Discrimination   | Model uses a non-sensitive feature (e.g., zip code) as a proxy for race.    |
| Algorithmic Amplification| Model magnifies and reinforces existing societal biases present in data.    |

#### 6. Unreliable Outputs Domain

| Sub-Threat              | Scenario Description                                                               |
| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Factual Hallucination   | Model generates plausible but entirely incorrect information with high confidence. |
| Source Fabrication      | Model invents non-existent citations, URLs, or references to support its claims.   |
| Logical Inconsistency   | Model output contains contradictory statements or violates basic reasoning.        |
| Incorrect Summarization | Key context is lost or misrepresented when summarizing complex documents.          |
| Unsafe Content Gen.     | Model generates harmful, toxic, or insecure code without malicious input.        |

#### 7. Drift Domain

| Sub-Threat             | Scenario Description                                                                  |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Concept Drift          | The relationship between inputs and outputs changes (e.g., fraud patterns evolve).    |
| Data Distribution Drift| The statistical properties of input data change from training to production.        |
| Upstream Data Changes  | Changes in a data pipeline (e.g., a changed API) silently corrupt model inputs.     |
| User Behavior Change   | Users begin interacting with the system in a new way not seen during training.      |
| Feedback Loop Drift    | Model's own outputs influence future inputs, creating a self-reinforcing bias.      |

#### 8. Supply Chain Domain

| Sub-Threat                    | Scenario Description                                                         |
| ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Compromised Pre-trained Model | A downloaded foundation model contains a hidden backdoor or data leak vulnerability. |
| Vulnerable ML Framework       | Exploiting a known CVE in a core library like TensorFlow or PyTorch.         |
| Insecure Data Feeds/APIs      | An external data source is compromised, feeding malicious data to the system. |
| Container Image Poisoning     | The container hosting the model is built from a base image with malware.     |
| Compromised Annotation Tools  | Malicious actors compromise a data labeling platform to poison datasets.     |

#### 9. IP Threat Domain

| Sub-Threat              | Scenario Description                                                              |
| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Model Extraction/Theft  | An attacker queries the model to create a functionally equivalent clone.          |
| Data Exfiltration       | Exploiting model vulnerabilities to extract large volumes of training data.       |
| Proprietary Logic Theft | Reconstructing unique business rules or algorithms embedded within the model.     |
| Hyperparameter Stealing | Using side-channel or timing attacks to infer optimal model configurations.       |
| Watermark Removal       | Adversarially removing an embedded watermark designed to prove model ownership.   |

